{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואך", :translit "v'ach", :gloss "but indeed"} {:he "ואך אם טמאה ארץ אחוזתכם", :translit "v'ach im t'me'ah eretz achuzatchem", :gloss "but indeed if the land of your possession is unclean"} {:he "ואך את הדבר", :translit "v'ach et ha-davar", :gloss "but indeed the matter"} {:he "ואך את דמכם לנפשותיכם", :translit "v'ach et dimchem l'nafshotaichem", :gloss "but indeed your blood for your souls"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ואך" "ואך אם טמאה ארץ אחוזתכם" "ואך את הדבר"]} {:kind :other, :note "Gezerah shavah across the three occurrences of ואך to derive that self-cursing is forbidden like self-killing, and that God demands justice even for speech and from all animals."}], :frame {:text "The verse Genesis 9:5 records God's demand for accounting for shed human blood. The Ba'al HaTurim links this to the prohibition against self-harm: just as one may not kill oneself, one may not curse oneself, deriving this from the repeated word ואך across three verses. The broader tradition that one may not harm oneself is addressed in rabbinic literature (Bava Kamma 91b), and the gravity of cursing is treated in Shabbat 105a. The phrase את דמכם לנפשותיכם in Genesis 9:5 establishes the demand for blood-accounting.", :sources ["Genesis 9:5" "Bava Kamma 91b" "Shabbat 105a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["ואך" "ואך אם טמאה ארץ אחוזתכם" "ואך את הדבר"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ואך", :refs ["Genesis 9:5" "Exodus 9:15" "Numbers 22:20" "Joshua 22:19"], :n 4, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ואך אם טמאה ארץ אחוזתכם", :refs ["Joshua 22:19"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ואך את הדבר", :refs ["Numbers 22:20"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Gezerah shavah across the three occurrences of ואך to derive that self-cursing is forbidden like self-killing, and that God demands justice even for speech and from all animals.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :b